## 0. Context you must internalize first

Phases 10–13 made auth, bot persistence, historical/paper/walk-forward/Monte-Carlo backtesting, the scheduled live paper-trading loop, and real Razorpay billing/credits all genuinely real. Read `docs/Full context.md` §9 and `docs/Prompt History.md` for how you got here.

**The gap this phase closes:** every node in `apps/engine/app/nodes/` — including the flagship `TechnicalAnalystNode` (`apps/engine/app/nodes/intelligence_agents/technical_analyst.py`) — is pure Python rule logic (RSI/EMA threshold checks). Nothing in the engine has ever called an LLM. Yet:
- The frontend's Deep Customization dialog (`components/builder/deep-customization-dialog.tsx`) already lets a user pick a provider/model/system prompt/temperature/max tokens for agent nodes (see `mock/models.ts`'s `PROVIDERS` and `mock/layers.ts`'s `ModelSelection` type).
- `technical_analyst.py` already reads `cfg.get("model", {...})` and writes it into `audit.model_config` — it just never uses it to actually call anything.
- The credit system you built in Phase 13 (`credit_wallets`, `credit_transactions`) exists specifically to be metered against something. Right now nothing debits credits for intelligence usage.

**Your job:** build a real, provider-agnostic LLM gateway in the engine, wire it into exactly one node — `TechnicalAnalystNode` — end to end (config → real API call → structured signal → credit debit → audit trail → frontend display), and leave every other agent node untouched. This is the reference implementation later phases will copy for `sentiment-agent`, `macro-agent`, `flow-agent`, etc. Do not touch those other nodes in this phase.

**Critical design constraint, decided for you — do not relitigate it:** LLM calls must **never** run during `historical` backtests. A 2-year, 15-minute-resolution historical backtest is ~70,000 bars; calling a real LLM on every bar would be prohibitively slow and expensive, and it violates the project's own stated rule ("Deterministic data first; LLM reasoning second" — `docs/Full context.md` §0, and the 4-stage validation pipeline in §9.1, which explicitly keeps Stage 1 historical backtesting to "deterministic features + ML ensemble only" and only introduces LLM reasoning at Stage 2, paper trading). Therefore:
- `historical` and `walk-forward` and `monte-carlo` backtest types: `TechnicalAnalystNode` always uses its existing deterministic RSI/EMA rule path, unchanged, regardless of the node's configured model. Do not call the LLM gateway from these paths, ever.
- `paper` backtest type and real scheduled `live` sessions (the only two places actual wall-clock time passes one bar at a time): if the node's configured `model.providerId` is not `"rule-engine"`, attempt a real LLM call; fall back to the deterministic rule on any failure.

This mirrors the product's own 4-stage philosophy exactly — you are not inventing new behavior, you are finally implementing what `docs/Full context.md` already specifies.

